Subject: including coordinates as fixed effects in a spatial model?
Message-ID: <AmIlslDHUK0.qHrP3FluAAAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/AmIlslDHUK0/m/qHrP3FluAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I had a question about including the x or y component of a coordinate as a fixed effect in an INLA model that already includes a spatial random effect. Is that somewhat redundant to include in a model like this? I guess I've seen a few analyses now where latitude or longitude (or both) were also used as fixed effects, and I was considering using latitude in my own analysis to understand how laying date varies with latitude and temperature anomaly at that particular location (whether it was a warm or cold spring for example). Is that latitude effect already captured when I include the spatial field in the model?

From uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com  Wed Jul  1 22:17:59 2020
From: uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com (Finn Lindgren)
Date: Wed, 01 Jul 2020 22:17:59 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] including coordinates as fixed effects in a spatial model?
Message-ID: <AmIlslDHUK0.i0LqAS_TAAAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <AmIlslDHUK0.qHrP3FluAAAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <AmIlslDHUK0.qHrP3FluAAAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/AmIlslDHUK0/m/i0LqAS_TAAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Jason,

this depends a bit on the situation; both options can be sensible.
With x&y as explicit covariates, the spatial random field component
can focus on non-linear effects, with a shorter correlation range,
than if x&y are left out, and the field has to capture both large
scale spatial linear trends, needing a long correlation range, as well
as smaller features.  But if there are large scale non-linear features
that are so large that they can't really be distinguished from the
linear trends, then having both x&y and the field may make the
coefficients non-identifiable.  This is normally not an issue in
prediction/smoothing settings, but requires extra care if the
parameters themselves are of interest.  Sometimes one can try to
impose trend-equal-zero constraints on the spatial field component,
but that isn't always ideal.

Personally, if I suspect there to be possible latitude and/or
east/west effects, I would include x&y as explicit covariates, and
then treat the combined effect of x&y&field as a single component, and
not try to tease apart the individual contributions.

Finn

